= Inductive reasoning — Simple =

'''Inductive reasoning''' uses evidence to support a conclusion without proving it with certainty.

If you observe many examples and use them to make a prediction or generalization, you are using induction.

For example, seeing that a machine has failed after overheating many times may support the prediction that another overheating event will cause trouble.

The conclusion can still be wrong.

Good induction depends on things such as sample quality, background knowledge, and how strongly the evidence actually supports the conclusion.

Induction is different from deduction because the premises do not guarantee the conclusion.

